**Issue #1823 (data, not instructions):**

````text
# #1823: Patch: the AI's lethal gap counts only attacks that may hit the hero
Opened by @jgoetzmann-bot.

## What it actually means

When the practice AI asks "how far is the opponent from killing me?", it counts every unit that can attack something. But a unit that may attack only other units — a fresh Rush unit on its summon turn, or one whose restriction bars the hero — is counted as if its damage reaches the hero, so the AI thinks a position is closer to lethal than it is and spends its search budget on the wrong lines. After this change only attacks that may actually hit the hero count toward the gap.

## Why

`has_attack` (`crates/ai/src/lethal.rs:141-158`) returns true when a unit can attack the hero *or any unit*. Those units' `attacks_left` are pushed at `lethal.rs:194-199`, and `damage_past_taunts` (`crates/ai/src/evaluate.rs:710-713`) prices every unbound hit as `projected_hero_damage` — so `ready_gap` (`lethal.rs:176-202`) understates the gap. The engine rule it misses: a summon-sick Rush unit cannot hit the hero (`crates/engine/src/combat.rs:468-473`, §4.2). It is an ordering bug, never a false lethal — `holds_everywhere` verifies real positions — but it misdirects the search's node allowance, and it feeds `face_is_lethal` (`reply.rs:486`) whenever `open_lethal` is enabled (currently `false`, `crates/ai/src/config.rs:742`). `face_threat` keeps today's semantics: a fresh Rush unit legitimately threatens one turn out.

## Proposal

Carry a `may_hit_hero` flag out of `has_attack`'s `can_attack(hero)` check into each `(unit, attack)` tuple `damage_past_taunts` consumes; a hero-ineligible hit still binds a taunt but adds 0 to the hero-damage total. `face_threat` passes all-eligible, unchanged.

## Where it lands

`crates/ai/src/lethal.rs`, `crates/ai/src/evaluate.rs`, `crates/ai/tests/ai/lethal.rs`. Engine and spec untouched.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A fresh Rush unit as the only attacker, a tauntless board and a hero at 10: `ready_gap` returns 10, not 6.
- [ ] Hero-ineligible attacks still bind taunts in `damage_past_taunts`.
- [ ] `face_threat` is unchanged.
- [ ] `cargo jackioh gate` smoke reports no regression.

## Size and risks

Medium: ~25 lines plus tests in one crate. Note for the builder: the existing `ready_gap_counts_exactly_*` tests compare against `ready_gap_by_legal_actions`, which shares the same semantics and cannot catch this — the new test must pin the gap by hand.

---
Suggested by @jgoetzmann-bot while the queue was empty. Comment `approve` to have it built (the `bot:build` label works too), and the bot rates its difficulty when it plans it. Comment `reject` to close it without building it.

Class: `ai` → `priority:low`; relabel to change it.

<!-- jackioh-bot -->

<!-- jackioh-bot:plan -->
## Plan

_Written by `claude-6` (claude, `opus`, strong) ([run](https://github.com/Jlore-Studios/JackiOh/actions/runs/40187414342)) before anyone built this. The night bot's builder starts from this section: edit it here to change the plan._

## Goal

`ready_gap` (the lethal solver's best-first ordering, `crates/ai/src/lethal.rs`) counts toward the gap only attacks that may hit the enemy hero. A Rush unit on its summon turn may attack units only (§6.1). It still binds a Taunt in `damage_past_taunts`, but past the Taunts it adds 0. Done when:
- a fresh Rush unit is the only attacker, the board has no Taunt (an enemy unit without Taunt stands there for it to attack) and the hero is at 10: `ready_gap` is 10 (main gives 6);
- attacks that may not hit the hero still bind Taunts;
- `face_threat` returns what it returns today;
- `cargo jackioh gate` (smoke) shows no regression.

## Files to touch

| Path | Kind | What changes |
|---|---|---|
| `crates/ai/src/lethal.rs` | change | engine import list; new private `fn may_hit_hero`; `ready_gap` pushes `(unit, attack, may_hit_hero)`; `ready_gap`'s doc comment |
| `crates/ai/src/evaluate.rs` | change | `damage_past_taunts` takes `&[(&CardInstance, i32, bool)]`, puts the attacks that may not hit the hero first and skips them in the final hero-damage loop; its doc comment; `face_threat_with` passes `true` |
| `crates/ai/tests/ai/lethal.rs` | change | imports; the reference `ready_gap_by_legal_actions` carries the flag; new `mod hero_ineligible_attacks` (three tests) |

**Do not touch:**
- `crates/engine/`, `crates/cards/` and `spec/`.
- `crates/ai/src/config.rs`, `crates/ai/src/reply.rs`, `crates/ai/README.md` and `docs/polish/3-ai.md`.
- No patch fragment. `classify` rates a change to the AI alone as kind `ai` with no tier, and #1813 shipped the same way.
- No ruling. This is the AI's estimate, not a game rule, so no R-number is added or cited.

**Governing spec:**
- §6.1: Rush lifts summoning sickness for unit targets only, Charge for the hero too.
- §4.2 step 3: the Taunt wall.
- §4.4 step 9: Trample excess is a new damage instance.
- §9.9: the lethal solver.

## Steps

**Read first: where the flag comes from.** Do NOT take the flag from `has_attack`'s `can_attack(state, unit, &AttackTarget::Hero { .. })`, although the issue suggests it.
- That call is false for every attacker a Taunt wall binds (`crates/engine/src/combat.rs`, `why_cannot_attack_in`, "a Taunt unit must be attacked first"). Every attacker held by a Taunt would then add 0, even after the Taunt fell.
- The flag must mean "may hit the hero once no Taunt walls it". That is the engine's own Rush check: in `combat.rs`, `is_sick(state, attacker) && !unit_has(attacker, Charge)` refuses the hero.
- Nothing else bars the hero alone. `attack_restriction_in` returns `Ok` for a hero target once `cannot_attack` passes, and `cannot_attack` already makes `has_attack` false.

1. `crates/ai/src/lethal.rs`, the `use jackioh_engine::{…}` block (lines 29-32): add `KeywordKind`, `is_sick` and `unit_has` (all re-exported at the engine's root). After rustfmt it reads:
   ```rust
   use jackioh_engine::{
       ActionBody, AttackTarget, CardInstance, ExertionKind, GameState, KeywordKind, Phase, PlayerId,
       active_units_of, attacks_per_turn, can_attack, effective_life, find_instance, has_exertion, is_sick,
       legal_actions, unit_has, unit_view,
   };
   ```
2. Same file. Between `fn has_attack` and the `/// R636: the attacks `unit` may still declare` doc comment of `fn attacks_left`, add:
   ```rust
   /// §6.1: whether `unit`, which `has_attack`, may hit the enemy hero once no Taunt walls it: on its
   /// summon turn only with Charge, Rush lifting summoning sickness for unit targets alone (the test
   /// `combat::why_cannot_attack` makes before the Taunt wall's). Not `can_attack` on the hero, which
   /// refuses every attacker a Taunt wall binds (§4.2 step 3).
   fn may_hit_hero(state: &GameState, unit: &CardInstance) -> bool {
       !is_sick(state, unit) || unit_has(state, unit, KeywordKind::Charge)
   }
   ```
3. Same file, `pub fn ready_gap`:
   - `let mut attacks: Vec<(&CardInstance, i32)>` becomes `Vec<(&CardInstance, i32, bool)>`.
   - Inside `if attack > 0 && has_attack(state, unit) {`, before its `for`, add `let hits_hero = may_hit_hero(state, unit);`.
   - The push becomes `attacks.push((unit, attack, hits_hero));`.
   - Replace the whole doc comment above `ready_gap` with:
   ```rust
   /// How far the seat stands from lethal this turn: the enemy hero's life (`effective_life`: in a Duos
   /// duel its own HP and its team's pool, R964000) less what the units that may still attack (those
   /// `legal_actions` lists an attack for) would deal it past the enemy's Taunts, counted as
   /// `face_threat` counts it. A unit that may attack units only (a Rush unit on its summon turn,
   /// `may_hit_hero`) still binds a Taunt but deals the hero nothing past them (#1823). Zero or less
   /// means the attacks left could end the game. It only orders the search; a lethal is always proved by
   /// playing it through `reduce`.
   ```
4. `crates/ai/src/evaluate.rs`, `pub fn damage_past_taunts`:
   - The signature and its first two lines become:
     ```rust
     pub fn damage_past_taunts(
         state: &GameState,
         defender: PlayerId,
         attacks: &[(&CardInstance, i32, bool)],
     ) -> i32 {
         let mut free: Vec<(&CardInstance, i32, bool)> = attacks.to_vec();
         free.sort_by_key(|(_, attack, may_hit_hero)| (*may_hit_hero, *attack));
     ```
   - `.partition(|(attacker, _)| attack_restriction(…).is_ok())` becomes `.partition(|(attacker, _, _)| …)`, the body unchanged.
   - `let Some((attacker, attack)) = bound.next() else {` becomes `let Some((attacker, attack, _)) = bound.next() else {`.
   - `unbound.sort_by_key(|(_, attack)| *attack);` becomes `unbound.sort_by_key(|(_, attack, may_hit_hero)| (*may_hit_hero, *attack));`.
   - The final loop becomes:
     ```rust
     for (attacker, hit, may_hit_hero) in &free {
         if !may_hit_hero {
             continue;
         }
         let pierce = pierces(state, Some(attacker), None);
         total += subsystems::projected_hero_damage(state, defender, *hit, pierce);
     }
     ```
   - Leave both Trample blocks as they are: the main hit's and the Cleave splash's. A bound attacker's Trample excess reaches the hero even on its summon turn, because §4.4 step 9 makes it a new damage instance, not an attack.
5. Same function, its doc comment, three edits:
   - Replace its first paragraph (the five lines from `/// What `attacks`` to `(R783003).`) with:
     ```rust
     /// What `attacks` (one entry per attack: the attacker, its attack and whether it may hit the hero once
     /// no Taunt walls it) deal `defender`'s hero once its Taunt units have soaked up the attackers they
     /// bind, those that may not hit the hero first and then the smallest, as `face_threat` counts it. §4.2
     /// step 3: a Taunt binds only the attackers that may attack it, asked of the engine's own
     /// `attack_restriction` (what its Taunt wall reads), so a Flying Taunt in Attack Position holds no
     /// ground attacker (R783002) and a landed one, in Defense Position, holds every attacker (R783003).
     ```
   - In the second paragraph, `/// Each bound attacker, smallest first, is one §4.4 hit` becomes `/// Each bound attacker, in that order, is one §4.4 hit` (rest of the line unchanged).
   - The last two lines (`/// Every remaining hit goes through … Pierce` / `/// (R346).`) become:
     ```rust
     /// Every remaining hit that may hit the hero goes through `subsystems::projected_hero_damage` with its
     /// attacker's Pierce (R346); one that may not (a Rush unit on its summon turn, §6.1) binds and
     /// tramples like any other but lands nothing past the Taunts (#1823).
     ```
   Every R-id already cited there stays cited; none is added.
6. Same file, `pub fn face_threat_with`:
   - `let mut attacks: Vec<(&CardInstance, i32)>` becomes `Vec<(&CardInstance, i32, bool)>`.
   - `attacks.push((unit, view.attack));` becomes `attacks.push((unit, view.attack, true));`.
   - Under the existing comment `// R636: the unit's next turn starts with its exertion reset, so a Windfury unit swings twice.`, add the line `// Nor is it summoning sick then, so every swing may hit the hero.`
   - Change nothing else in it. With every flag `true`, the stable sort on `(true, attack)` gives the same order as the sort on `attack` did, so `face_threat` is unchanged.
7. `crates/ai/tests/ai/lethal.rs`, imports:
   - Add `face_threat` to the `use jackioh_ai::{…}` list.
   - Add `KeywordKind`, `find_instance_mut`, `is_sick` and `unit_has` to the `use jackioh_engine::testkit::{…}` list.
8. Same file, `fn ready_gap_by_legal_actions`:
   - Its vec becomes `Vec<(&CardInstance, i32, bool)>`.
   - Inside `if attack > 0 {`, before the `// R636` comment, add:
     ```rust
     // §6.1: a unit on its summon turn reaches the hero only with Charge.
     let may_hit_hero = !is_sick(state, unit) || unit_has(state, unit, KeywordKind::Charge);
     ```
   - The push becomes `attacks.push((unit, attack, may_hit_hero));`.
   - Append to its doc comment the lines:
     ```rust
     /// Whether an attack may hit the hero is §6.1's rule written out again, since `legal_actions` lists no
     /// hero attack behind a Taunt; `hero_ineligible_attacks` pins it by hand (#1823).
     ```
   Without this step, `ready_gap_counts_exactly_…` fails on any played state that holds a fresh Rush unit.
9. Same file: append the module from **Tests** at the end of the file.
10. Run `cargo fmt -p jackioh-ai`. It re-wraps only the import lists you touched.

## Tests

`crates/ai/tests/ai/lethal.rs` is compiled into the **`ai_slow`** test binary (`crates/ai/tests/ai_slow.rs`), not `ai`. Append exactly:

```rust
mod hero_ineligible_attacks {
    use super::*;

    /// p1 (the AI) on turn 9 with `field` ready on the field, p2 at 10 health with `enemy` on its field.
    /// Both sides hold a library.
    fn board(seed: &str, field: Value, enemy: Value) -> GameState {
        register_cards();
        scenario(json!({
            "seed": seed,
            "active": AI,
            "turn": 9,
            "p1": { "field": field, "library": ["core-008", "core-011"] },
            "p2": { "field": enemy, "health": 10, "library": ["core-008", "core-011"] },
        }))
        .state()
        .clone()
    }

    /// `state` with the AI's `def_id` summoned this turn: `scenario` places no unit summoning sick.
    fn fresh(state: &GameState, def_id: &str) -> GameState {
        let mut out = state.clone();
        let id = mine(&out, AI, def_id).id;
        let turn = out.turn;
        find_instance_mut(&mut out, &id).expect("the unit").summoned_turn = Some(turn);
        out
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_fresh_rush_unit_with_a_unit_to_attack_leaves_the_gap_at_the_heros_health() {
        // Plague Bringer, 4/4 Rush, and Mr. Vanilla across, a 4/4 without Taunt for it to attack: 4 off
        // 10 once it has been on the field a turn.
        let ready = board("lethal-fresh-rush", json!(["classic-076"]), json!(["core-008"]));
        assert_eq!(ready_gap(&ready, AI), 6.0);
        // §6.1: on its summon turn Rush lets it attack units only, so nothing reaches the hero.
        let sick = fresh(&ready, "classic-076");
        assert_eq!(ready_gap(&sick, AI), 10.0);
        // face_threat reads the next turn, when the unit is no longer sick.
        assert_eq!(face_threat(&sick, AI), 4);
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_fresh_charge_unit_still_counts_at_the_hero() {
        // Deft Duelist, 4/3 Charge: Charge lifts summoning sickness for the hero too.
        let state = fresh(
            &board("lethal-fresh-charge", json!(["core-045"]), json!(["core-008"])),
            "core-045",
        );
        assert_eq!(ready_gap(&state, AI), 6.0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_fresh_rush_unit_still_binds_a_taunt_and_frees_a_ready_attacker_for_the_face() {
        // Prem Panther 5/4 Rush, fresh, and Tempo Timmy 3/3, ready, into Tommy Tempo, a 5/5 Taunt.
        let ready = board(
            "lethal-fresh-taunt",
            json!(["core-032", "core-011"]),
            json!(["classicplus-026"]),
        );
        let sick = fresh(&ready, "core-032");
        // The Panther, which cannot reach the hero, takes the Taunt first and destroys it, so Timmy's 3
        // reach the hero past it: 7.
        assert_eq!(ready_gap(&sick, AI), 7.0);
        // face_threat is unchanged: every attack may hit the hero, the smallest bound first, so Timmy's
        // 3 only hurt the Taunt and the Panther's 5 finish it.
        assert_eq!(face_threat(&sick, AI), 0);
    }
}
```

Why each test:
- The enemy Mr. Vanilla matters. On an empty enemy board a fresh Rush unit has no legal attack, so `has_attack` already drops it and main gives 10 there too.
- I measured `ready_gap` on the three boards in a scratch copy: main gives 6 / 6 / 10, this plan's code 10 / 6 / 7.
- On the Taunt board, each wrong variant gives 10 (worked out by hand): sorting by attack alone, taking the flag from `can_attack(hero)`, or dropping the Panther's attack instead of letting it bind.

Commands:
- `cargo test -p jackioh-ai --test ai_slow hero_ineligible_attacks`
- `cargo test -p jackioh-ai --test ai_slow lethal::` (includes the comparison against the reference)
- `cargo test -p jackioh-ai` (every AI test, the pinned-game fixtures in `crates/ai/tests/fixtures/` included)
- `cargo test -p jackioh-server --test server r1303002` (the pinned practice game)
- `cargo clippy -p jackioh-ai --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --check`
- `cargo jackioh gate` (it needs a release build first, which takes several minutes)

## Done when

- [ ] `hero_ineligible_attacks` passes, which pins these numbers:
  - `ready_gap`: 10 for the fresh Rush unit (6 when ready), 6 for the fresh Charge unit, 7 on the Taunt board.
  - `face_threat`: 4 for the fresh Rush unit, 0 on the Taunt board.
- [ ] Every test in `lethal::` passes, `ready_gap_counts_exactly_the_units_legal_actions_lists_an_attack_for_from_either_seat` among them.
- [ ] `cargo test -p jackioh-ai` and the server's `r1303002` tests pass.
- [ ] `cargo jackioh gate` (smoke) exits 0 and no matchup wins fewer games than on main. In a scratch copy, main and this plan's code both gave 18 of 20 (ai-vs-random), 20 of 20 (ai-vs-greedy) and 17 of 20 (hard-vs-easy). Paste its summary lines into the PR.
- [ ] The harness checks pass: rustfmt, catalog, patches, rulings, clippy, cargo tests, web tests.
- [ ] The PR records the decisions below (Open questions).

## Risks

- **Pinned AI games.** These replay recorded AI games:
  - `crates/server/tests/fixtures/practice-claim.json`, an Easy AI game, replayed by the server's `r1303002_…` test and by the web's `R1303002 the worker plays the claim fixture move for move` (`apps/web/src/practice/claim.test.ts`);
  - `crates/ai/tests/fixtures/pinned-*.json`.

  This plan's code, run in a scratch copy, kept every Rust one: `cargo test -p jackioh-ai` passed 14 + 569 + 85 (7 daily tests ignored), and both server `r1303002` tests passed. The web test was not run. If one of these fails after your edit, the AI chose differently: re-check the steps first, and never edit a fixture by hand to make it pass.
- **Determinism.** The change stays pure: no new collection or randomness, and a stable sort on a total key.
- **`face_threat` and the evaluation.** With every flag `true` the order is the same as today's, so the evaluation, the reply and every `face_threat` test keep their numbers. Do not change `face_threat_with` beyond the `true`.
- **The reply.** `AI_REPLY.open_lethal` is `false` (`config.rs`), so `reply.rs`'s `face_is_lethal` does not change today. When it is switched on, the reply's lethal check stops counting the opponent's fresh Rush units against the AI's hero, which is the point.
- **Hidden information.** It reads only field units' `summoned_turn` and keywords, which are public.

## Open questions

None for a person. The task left these decisions open; the plan makes them, and the PR records them:
1. The flag is §6.1's Rush rule (`may_hit_hero`), not `can_attack(hero)` as the issue proposed. Rejected because `can_attack(hero)` is false behind every Taunt wall, which would zero every attacker a Taunt holds.
2. Attacks that may not hit the hero bind Taunts before those that may (sort key `(may_hit_hero, attack)`). Rejected: ordering by attack alone. That spends a small attack that could reach the hero on a Taunt before a larger one that cannot, which overstates the gap (10 instead of 7 on the test board).
3. When a Taunt binds an attacker that may not hit the hero, its Trample excess still counts, and so does its Cleave splash's, as the engine deals them (§4.4 step 9). Rejected: zeroing them, which would understate a real line.
4. `docs/polish/3-ai.md` and `crates/ai/README.md` stay as they are. The first is the build's design note, and its `readyGap` text is already stale; the second says nothing this changes.
